{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2160-11-04 00:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Patel. You're in the hospital because you had chest pain while in the ER. Can you tell me what happened?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2160-11-04 00:47:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yeah, I’ve been drinking a lot—like, several quarts of vodka and beer—plus using cocaine and heroin for about five days. I was under a bridge. Then my chest started hurting, real sharp in the center, didn’t go away with the nitro spray I sometimes use."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2160-11-04 00:50:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay, thanks for sharing. Your alcohol level was quite high when you arrived—355—and your urine test showed opioids but no cocaine. The chest pain wasn’t from a heart attack—we checked your troponins twice and they were normal, and your EKG didn’t show new changes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2160-11-04 00:53:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not my heart? But I’ve had stents before—four of them. I’m scared."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2160-11-04 00:55:20",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I understand. You do have serious heart disease, and the EKG still shows old damage from a past heart attack. But this episode doesn’t look like a new one. The pain might be from withdrawing from alcohol and heroin. You mentioned tremors and diarrhea—those are withdrawal signs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2160-11-04 00:58:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yeah, I’ve been shaking bad. Haven’t had a drink or anything since yesterday. Is that dangerous?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2160-11-04 01:00:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It can be. That’s why we started you on a medication called Valium every few hours—it helps with withdrawal and prevents seizures. We’re also watching your blood pressure and heart rate, which are a bit high—154 over 111, heart rate 106."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2160-11-04 01:03:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I didn’t take my blood pressure pills lately. I lost them. But I take lisinopril and metoprolol usually."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2160-11-04 01:05:40",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We restarted your lisinopril. But we’re holding the metoprolol for now—because of the recent cocaine use, it could be risky. We’ll talk to your primary doctor about restarting it later."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2160-11-04 05:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. We repeated your blood work, including another troponin—it’s still negative. Your CK level was a bit high, but that’s likely from muscle strain or dehydration, not heart damage."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2160-11-04 05:33:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So I’m okay to go? I feel shaky but no chest pain now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2160-11-04 05:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Medically, you’re stable, but you’re still in withdrawal. Leaving now carries risks—like seizures, heart issues, or even death. We’d really prefer you stay for full detox."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2160-11-04 05:39:10",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I know, but I need to get out. I’ve been here before. Can I at least get some help for rehab?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2160-11-04 05:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, social work spoke with you and gave info on two rehab programs—both might have openings tomorrow. Please call them. We also started a new medication—Imdur, a long-acting nitrate—to help with blood pressure and possible angina."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2160-11-04 05:45:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So I’m stopping metoprolol and starting Imdur? What about my other meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2160-11-04 05:48:15",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll keep your aspirin, Plavix, pravastatin, lisinopril, citalopram, and clonazepam. The oxycodone at night is still okay. But no more cocaine—mixing it with metoprolol is dangerous."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2160-11-04 15:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see you’ve decided to leave against medical advice. I want to make sure you understand: withdrawal symptoms can get worse, and your heart is already weak. Please call your primary doctor and those rehab centers."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2160-11-04 15:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I hear you. I’ll make the calls tomorrow. I don’t want to keep doing this—it’s killing me."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2160-11-04 15:05:45",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a good step. We’re giving you a list of your meds and instructions. If chest pain returns, or you feel confused or have a seizure, go back to the ER immediately."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2160-11-04 15:08:20",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. Thanks for not giving up on me, even though I’m leaving early."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2160-11-04 15:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We care about your health. Please take care of yourself. You’re strong enough to make a change."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2160-11-04 15:12:30",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll try. I really will."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2160-11-04 15:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "One more thing—your stool was black and green. That could be from something you ate, but if it stays dark or you feel weak, get it checked. Could be stomach bleeding."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2160-11-04 15:17:45",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I ate some weird food under the bridge. I’ll watch it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2160-11-04 15:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Fair enough. Just be careful. Here’s your medication list and contact numbers. Please call someone tomorrow."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2160-11-04 15:23:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thank you, doc."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2160-11-04 15:25:30",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take care. We’re here if you need us."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2160-11-04 16:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m heading out now. I’ll call the rehab place first thing."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2160-11-04 00:36:00",
    "admission_end": "2160-11-04 16:40:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient was admitted with chest pain following a five-day polysubstance binge involving alcohol, cocaine, and heroin. He had a high blood alcohol level and positive urine toxicology for opioids. Evaluation ruled out acute myocardial infarction with normal troponins and unchanged EKG. The chest pain was deemed atypical and likely related to alcohol and opioid withdrawal, though hypertension may have contributed. He was treated with Valium for withdrawal, restarted on lisinopril and other cardiac medications, and started on Imdur for blood pressure. Metoprolol was held due to recent cocaine use. The patient decided to leave against medical advice after being counseled on the risks. He was provided with rehab resources and instructed to follow up with his primary care physician.",
    "problems": [
      "Alcohol withdrawal",
      "Acute alcoholic intoxication in alcoholism, continuous",
      "Drug withdrawal",
      "Opioid type dependence, continuous",
      "Cocaine abuse, continuous",
      "Other chest pain",
      "Coronary atherosclerosis of native coronary artery",
      "Percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty status",
      "Dysthymic disorder",
      "Unspecified viral hepatitis C without hepatic coma",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Pure hypercholesterolemia"
    ]
  }
}
